 ### [Occlusion Culling Granularity](1671-occlusion-culling-granularity.md)
-Configures the spatial resolution for raster occlusion culling to match the leaf-level granularity of a reference ray-marching octree. It ensures that the culling proxy geometry aligns with the underlying extraction data structures for direct performance comparison.
+Configures the spatial resolution for raster occlusion culling to match the leaf-level granularity of a reference ray-marching octree. It ensures that the culling proxy geometry aligns with the underlying extraction data structures for direct performance comparison. Choose this variant when precise granularity matching between culling and extraction is required.
 
 ### [Volumetric Occlusion Culling via Occlusion Queries](4594-volumetric-occlusion-culling-via-occlusion-queries.md)
-Determines if a voxel block is visible by rasterizing its bounding box and checking for fragment depth test passes. It differs from standard binary occlusion culling by incorporating accumulated opacity into the depth test, ensuring that blocks behind fully opaque foreground volumes are correctly culled.
+Determines if a voxel block is visible by rasterizing its bounding box and checking for fragment depth test passes. It differs from standard binary occlusion culling by incorporating accumulated opacity into the depth test, ensuring that blocks behind fully opaque foreground volumes are correctly culled. Choose this variant when opacity-aware culling is necessary.
 
 ### [Hierarchical Raster Occlusion Culling](6061-hierarchical-raster-occlusion-culling.md)
-A fully GPU-driven technique that reduces rasterization overhead by performing visibility tests on a coarse hierarchy of group nodes rather than individual leaf objects, refining results via ray traversal to the leaf level. It distinguishes itself from standard Raster Occlusion Culling by replacing the exhaustive rasterization of all leaf proxies with a hierarchical culling pass, and from Iterative Raster Occlusion Culling by utilizing a hybrid raster-ray approach for full hierarchy descent.
+A fully GPU-driven technique that reduces rasterization overhead by performing visibility tests on a coarse hierarchy of group nodes rather than individual leaf objects, refining results via ray traversal to the leaf level. It distinguishes itself from standard Raster Occlusion Culling by replacing the exhaustive rasterization of all leaf proxies with a hierarchical culling pass, and from Iterative Raster Occlusion Culling by utilizing a hybrid raster-ray approach for full hierarchy descent. Choose this variant for scenes with deep hierarchies to minimize rasterization costs.
 
 ### Hierarchical Visibility Mask
-A visibility culling technique where triangles are marked as visible and forwarded to graphics hardware rather than being rendered by the CPU, allowing the GPU to determine actual visibility and handle occlusion [S5, book]. It projects the polygon onto the top meta-tile of a hierarchical structure and recursively checks only those subtiles in which the polygon might be visible, leading to a logarithmic search [S5, book]. If a meta-cell size is approximately the size of a single pixel, it stops recursion and forwards a single point with an associated normal to the graphics hardware [S5, book]. The normal is estimated by calculating the gradient of the field [S5, book]. It differs from Greene's implementation by not rendering the visible portion on the CPU; instead, it relies on the graphics accelerator to determine visible pieces and render them correctly [S5, book].
+A visibility culling technique where triangles are marked as visible and forwarded to graphics hardware rather than being rendered by the CPU, allowing the GPU to determine actual visibility and handle occlusion [S5, book]. It projects the polygon onto the top meta-tile of a hierarchical structure and recursively checks only those subtiles in which the polygon might be visible, leading to a logarithmic search [S5, book]. If a meta-cell size is approximately the size of a single pixel, it stops recursion and forwards a single point with an associated normal to the graphics hardware [S5, book]. The normal is estimated by calculating the gradient of the field [S5, book]. It differs from Greene's implementation by not rendering the visible portion on the CPU; instead, it relies on the graphics accelerator to determine visible pieces and render them correctly [S5, book]. Choose this variant when CPU rendering overhead must be avoided.
+
+### Cluster Visibility Buffer Culling
+A culling technique using a visibility buffer to prune non-visible clusters, performing back-face and occlusion culling without requiring a hierarchical Z-buffer or storing cluster bounds/normal cones [S6]. It avoids full cell rasterisation of clusters that are fully occluded or backfacing by storing visibility bits per cluster [S6]. Choose this variant when working with clustered grid cell data structures and deferred splatting pipelines.
 
